<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwidzyn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marienwerder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwidzyn, German Marienwerder, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> near the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> founded a castle there in 1233 during its conquest of Pomesania, and it became the seat of the cathedral chapter of the Bishopric of Pomesania, whose brick Gothic castle and cathedral of the 14th century still stand. It remained German until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwidzyn.jpg" alt="Kwidzyn" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karina Pietuszyńska · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Budynek_Urz%C4%99du_Miejskiego_i_Rejonowego_w_Kwidzynie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kwidzyn lies on the Liwa River, about 5 kilometres east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and some 70 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, in the region of Powiśle. A Pomesanian settlement called Kwedis existed there in the 11th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> then built a castle and founded the town of Marienwerder, which was peopled with artisans and traders from towns in the northern parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1243 the Bishopric of Pomesania received the town and castle from the Order as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, and the settlement became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Pomesania. The knight Werner von Orseln, murdered at Marienburg in 1330, was among the first to be buried in the new cathedral. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorothea-of-montau">Saint Dorothea of Montau</a> lived in the town from 1391 until her death in 1394, and the pilgrims to her shrine helped its economy. The Prussian Confederation, which opposed Teutonic rule, was founded there on 14 March 1440. In 1454 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> incorporated the region at its request, and the Thirteen Years' War began. After the Order's defeat the town became part of Poland in 1466, held as a fief by the Order.</p><p>The Teutonic state became a secular Lutheran duchy in 1525, and in 1618 the ducal rights passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. Brandenburg severed its ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> in 1657 and made its realm the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701. In 1772 the district of Marienwerder joined the new Prussian Province of West Prussia. The town had 12,983 inhabitants in 1910, of whom 12,408 were German-speaking and 346 Polish-speaking.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the town lay in an area that voted in a plebiscite supervised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. In Marienwerder 7,811 votes were cast to remain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and 362 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and Polish sources report electoral fraud. In January 1945 the civilians were ordered to evacuate, and most left. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured the town on 30 January 1945 and burned and pillaged its centre. Settlers from Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> later repopulated it, and it returned to Poland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kwidzyn is the capital of Kwidzyn County in the Pomeranian Voivodeship and has 36,731 inhabitants. Its main landmark is the 14th-century Brick Gothic castle, seat of the Pomesanian Cathedral Chapter, which now houses a museum and is a Historic Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The adjacent co-cathedral of Saint John the Evangelist, built between 1343 and 1384, contains the tombs of three Grand Masters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kwidzyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1440 they founded the Prussian Confederation at <b>Marienwerder</b> (Kwidzyn), led by the citizens of Danzig, Elbing and Thorn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its conquest was secured by a dense network of castles, at first of timber and earth and later of brick, from Thorn and <b>Marienwerder</b> to Königsberg, founded in 1255 during a crusade led by King Ottokar II of Bohemia and named in his honour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Marienwerder</b>, a group of 53 nobles and clergy and 19 cities in Prussia formed the Prussian Confederation to oppose the arbitrariness of the Teutonic Knights.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishop received a third of the territory as his own principality, with the castle of Riesenburg (Prabuty) as residence and a cathedral at <b>Marienwerder</b> (Kwidzyn).</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-pomesania">Bishopric of Pomesania</a></li><li><blockquote>The Teutonic Order, invited by Konrad of Masovia, attacked Pomesania first, founding <b>Kwidzyn</b> (Marienwerder) in 1233 and winning at the Sirgune river in 1234; by 1236 the land was subdued.</blockquote><a href="/state/pomesanians">Pomesanians</a></li></ul></section>
